.TH UNLINK 2 "August 1, 1992"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
unlink \- remove directory entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
int unlink(const char *\fIpath\fP);
.ft R
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.IR unlink 
function removes the link whose pathname is specified by \fIpath\fP
and decrement the link count of the file referenced by the link.
If this entry was the last link to the file,
and no process has the file open, then
all resources associated with the file are reclaimed.
If, however, the file was open in any process, the actual
resource reclamation is delayed until it is closed,
even though the directory entry has disappeared.
.PP
The \fIpath\fP argument must not name a directory.
Applications should use
.IR rmdir 
to remove a directory.
.PP
Upon successful completion, the
.IR unlink 
function marks for update the \fIst_ctime\fP and \fIst_mtime\fP fields of
the parent directory of the entry to be removed.
Furthermore, if the link count of the file is not zero, then the
\fIst_ctime\fP field of the file is also marked for update.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Upon successful completion, a value of zero is returned.
Otherwise, a value of \-1 is returned and \fIerrno\fP
is set to indicate the error.
.SH "ERRORS"
If any of the following conditions occurs, the
.IR unlink 
function returns \-1 and sets \fIerrno\fP to the corresponding value:
.TP 15
[EACCES]
Search permission is denied for a component of the path prefix.
.TP 15
[EACCES]
Write permission is denied on the directory containing the link
to be removed.
.TP 15
[EBUSY]
The entry to be unlinked is the mount point for a
mounted file system.
.TP 15
[EFAULT]
The \fIpath\fP argument points outside the process's allocated address space.
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
The pathname contains a character with the high-order bit set.
.TP 15
[EIO]
An I/O error occurred while deleting the directory entry
or deallocating the inode.
.TP 15
[ELOOP]
Too many symbolic links were encountered in translating the pathname.
.TP 15
[ENAMETOOLONG]
A component of
.I path
exceeds 255 characters,
or the entire pathname exceeds 1023 characters.
For POSIX applications these values are given
by the constants {NAME_MAX} and {PATH_MAX}, respectively.
.TP 15
[ENOENT]
The named file does not exist.
.TP 15
[ENOENT]
The \fIpath\fP argument points to an empty string.
.TP 15
[ENOTDIR]
A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
.TP 15
[EPERM]
The named file is a directory.
.TP 15
[EPERM]
The directory containing the file is marked sticky,
and neither the containing directory nor the file to be removed
are owned by the effective user ID.
.TP 15
[EROFS]
The named file resides on a read-only file system.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
close(2),
link(2),
open(2),
rename(2),
rmdir(2)
